<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazimierz Michałowski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazimierz Michałowski was a Polish archaeologist and Egyptologist, founder of the Polish school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> archaeology. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> campaign to save the monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> his mission excavated the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> from 1961 to 1964 and rescued its wall paintings before the site was flooded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>; they are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>. He also began excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-dongola">Old Dongola</a> and worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazimierz-michalowski.jpg" alt="Kazimierz Michałowski" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harry Weinberg · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazimierz_Micha%C5%82owski_MNW_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kazimierz Józef Marian Michałowski was born in Tarnopol on 14 December 1901, the grandson of Emil Michałowski, the town's mayor. He studied classical archaeology and art history at the Jan Kazimierz University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a> and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and took part in French excavations at Delphi, Thasos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>. His doctorate of 1926, supervised by Edmund Bulanda, dealt with the Niobids in Greek art. In 1931 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Classical Archaeology at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, which he headed until he retired in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1936 he began excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> with the French Institute for Eastern Archaeology, the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world with Polish archaeologists. Taken prisoner in the September campaign of 1939, he lectured on Egyptology to fellow prisoners in the camp Oflag II-C Woldenburg. After the war he was dean and pro-rector at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and in 1960 opened the Polish Centre of Mediterranean Archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, which he headed until his death.</p><p>His teams worked at Myrmekion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, at Tell Atrib, and from 4 May 1959 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, where they found a hoard of jewellery and 27 gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a>. At Kom el-Dikka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> they uncovered the first ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a> a funerary temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>. He chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> experts who oversaw the cutting of the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> into 30-tonne blocks and their rebuilding, and from 1965 he dug at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nea-paphos">Nea Paphos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where a mosaic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> came to light.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Michałowski died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 1 January 1981 and was buried at Brwinów. He coined the term Nubiology for the study of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. The Polish Centre of Mediterranean Archaeology and the Faras Gallery of the National Museum in Warsaw bear his name, as do streets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Słupsk</a>, and his bust stands in the gardens of the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. He received honorary doctorates from Strasbourg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kazimierz Michałowski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The wall paintings of Faras cathedral, rescued by a Polish mission led by <b>Kazimierz Michałowski</b> before the area was flooded by Lake Nasser in the 1960s, are now in the National Museum in Warsaw and the Sudan National Museum in Khartoum and show bishops, kings and saints in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ternopil">Ternopil</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.57,25.6&amp;year=1953" aria-label="Ternopil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5139 437 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kazimierz Michałowski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5589 707h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5589 707h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.57,25.6&amp;year=1953">What was here in 1953? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
